title: "Claim records and what a coding agent says: four preregistered experiments — replication package"
type: software
version: "1.0.0"
url: "https://github.com/ulissesflores/eilikrineia-study/releases/tag/v1.0.0"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ulissesflores/eilikrineia-study"
authors:
  - family-names: "Flores"
    given-names: "Carlos Ulisses"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6034-7765"
    website: "https://ulissesflores.com"
    affiliation: "Codex Hash Research Laboratory, Codex Hash Ltda., Brazil"
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Four preregistered experiments (a pilot and three studies, 6,224 trials, Claude Haiku 5.5 as a
  coding agent on tasks with hidden checks) on whether a signed, hash-chained record of the
  agent's own claims changes what it claims and what it delivers. Publishing the record showed no
  detectable effect on false completion claims. Asking for a record with evidence (instruction,
  tool and signed record together) lowered false completion by 6.6 points and raised false
  non-completion by 27.9 points; a "complete" claim was not more often true (73.5% against 77.5%)
  and the share of finished work was about the same. Pressure framing and independent acceptance
  tests showed no gain on the confirmatory tests; the pressure comparison depends on a labeller
  whose accuracy differs between arms against a rater added post hoc. The completion labeller was
  not validated by a person.
keywords:
  - AI agents
  - coding agents
  - false completion claims
  - preregistration
  - negative result
  - hash-chained ledger
  - provenance
